@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+## Single-box deploy (Kamal + SQLite)
+
+Run Fizzy on one Linux server with Docker: one container serves the web app
+and runs background jobs, SQLite + uploads persist in a Docker volume, and
+the kamal-proxy terminates SSL (automatic Let's Encrypt certificates).
+
+### Prerequisites
+
+- A Linux server with your SSH key installed, ports 80/443 reachable.
+- DNS for your hostname (e.g. `fizzy.example.com`) pointing at the server.
+- A Docker Hub account (or another registry) with a private `fizzy` repository.
+- A mail server: magic-login codes are emailed, so SMTP is required.
+
+### Steps
+
+1. Fork this repo.
+
+2. Edit `config/deploy.yml` and replace the placeholders:
+   - `YOUR_SERVER_IP` → your server's IP/hostname.
+   - `YOUR_APP_HOST` (in `proxy/host`, `env/clear/APP_HOST`,
+     `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_DOMAIN`) → your hostname.
+     Fizzy will be at `https://YOUR_APP_HOST`.
+   - `your-user` (in `image`, `registry/username`) → your registry username.
+   - `env/clear` SMTP settings → your mail server
+     (`SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_USERNAME`, `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION`,
+     `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO`).
+
+3. Export secrets (never commit raw values; see `.kamal/secrets`):
+
+   ```sh
+   export KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=...  # registry token/password
+   export SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(openssl rand -hex 64)
+   export SMTP_PASSWORD=...            # password for SMTP_USERNAME
+   ```
+
+4. Deploy:
+
+   ```sh
+   bin/kamal setup   # first time: Docker, proxy, boot
+   bin/kamal deploy  # every next deploy
+   ```
+
+### How it works
+
+- SQLite databases (`storage/*.sqlite3`) and uploads
+  (`storage/production/files`) both live under `/rails/storage`, persisted in
+  the `fizzy_storage` volume — data survives the next deploy.
+- Background jobs run on the same box inside Puma via Solid Queue
+  (`SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA=true`); recurring jobs are in `config/recurring.yml`.
+- `APP_HOST` / `SMTP_*` are wired up in `config/environments/production.rb`;
+  `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` sets the From address (see `ApplicationMailer`).
